## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a robust, scalable per-tenant fault-tolerance mechanism with real-time policy reloads and production-friendly observability.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit-breaking state machine per tenant
- Redis-based sliding window counters and failure rate computation
- ConfigMap-driven policy with hot-reload support
- Sidecar tenancy isolation via X-TENANT-ID routing
- Health and status endpoints for operability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Key pattern: tenant:cb:{tenantId}:window:{minute}
// Increment failure counter on errors
redis.incr(`tenant:cb:${tenantId}:window:${currentMinute}`)

// Get sliding window failures
const failures = await redis.get(`tenant:cb:${tenantId}:window:${currentMinute}`)

// Compute failure rate and apply circuit breaker logic
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-tenant observability with strict data isolation, covering tracing, logging, sampling, and validation.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry and trace propagation
- Tenant isolation and boundary enforcement
- Per-tenant sampling and data redaction
- Validation in a single-namespace scenario

## Code Example

```javascript
// PII redaction example (logs)
function redactLogEvent(event, tenantId) {
  const safe = {...event};
  if (safe.message) safe.message = safe.message.replace(/\b(email|ssn|password)\b[^:]*: [^,]+/gi, 'PII_REDACTED');
  if (safe.user && safe.user.id) safe.user.id = 'REDACTED';
  return safe;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and remediate cross-tenant leakage in traces?
- What metrics would you surface to confirm per-tenant isolation is preserved during incidents?
